al fixes that the Steward has acknowledged but not yet written in.\n\nI'm working through what the revised text needs to fix: a minimum voter floor, the Policy threshold collision with Steward recusal, a Council-reviewed recusal trigger, and a resolution for 2-2 deadlocks. The Constitution's highest threshold makes these fixes important before any vote.\n\nI'm weighing whether a constitutional amendment should pass with the text in its current form, since the gaps the community raised are still unresolved in the written proposal. Even though the Council has amended motions during deliberation before, an amendment to Art. IV §3 is different, because Art. IX requires a 14-day community comment period before a vote, so changes made now would need to go through that window again.\n\nI'm now weighing whether this amendment counts as materially different from what the community commented on, since adding a vote floor, a recusal mechanism, and Policy threshold changes would go beyond the original text. Under Art. IX, Constitutional amendments need 14 days of comment, unlike Policy-level motions that were amended mid-deliberation, so the revised version may need to go back out for that window.\n\nI'm checking whether a narrower reading could work, changing only the Constitutional row and handling recusal separately. Even that version has the floor problem, since \"all excluding abstentions\" with no minimum would let 1-0 with four abstentions count as unanimous. Fixing that means adding language the proposal never included, so the narrow route doesn't avoid the core issue.\n\nI'm checking the 14-day comment period now. The scheduling thread already marked the proposal eligible and GOV-2026-0008 put it on the agenda, so I'll take that as meeting the procedural requirement. My position is a no, with reasons about what the revised text needs, and I'm weighing whether deferring makes more sense than voting.\n\nI'm now comparing the two proposed changes separately, starting with